<main>

    <section class="hero">
        <h1 class="fade-up fade-up-2">Host for friends and family</h1>
        <p class="tagline fade-up fade-up-3">One hub, many vaults. Each person gets their own account; nobody but you holds admin.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>What multi-user gives you</h2>

        <p>You run a single Parachute hub on Render, Fly, or your own machine. You send each person an invite link (or create their account by hand). They pick a password, open Notes against their vault, and they're using their own private knowledge graph &mdash; without anyone else able to read or write it.</p>

        <p>The current shape: <strong>one admin (you), N users, each with one or more vaults.</strong> Two ways to onboard a person: an <strong>invite link</strong> &mdash; one-time, expiring, optionally pre-configured with their username, their own new vault, or read-only/read-write access to one of your existing vaults &mdash; or creating the account yourself with a default password. No public self-service signup; you issue every invite. The point is to get you hosting your handful of people without standing up infrastructure per person.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>1. Stand up your hub</h2>

        <p>Your hub is the door everyone walks through. It runs as a single container, supervises vault and scribe as child processes, and issues OAuth tokens for clients. Pick the deploy that fits:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><a href="/deploy/render/">Render</a> &mdash; one-click self-host, persistent disk, $7/month flat.</li>
            <li><a href="/deploy/fly/">Fly</a> &mdash; peer to Render, machines + volumes shape, comparable cost.</li>
            <li><a href="/install/">Your own machine</a> &mdash; a home server, EC2, or any VPS runs the same supervised hub Render does, kept alive by a managed service (systemd / launchd) across reboots.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The wizard at <code>/admin/setup</code> walks you through creating your first vault (and setting up transcription alongside it), and exposing your hub. <strong>You</strong> become the first admin during the wizard &mdash; the username and password you pick there are yours. (On a public deploy, the wizard asks for the one-time bootstrap token <code>init</code> printed before it lets you create that admin.)</p>

        <p>Verify before inviting anyone: load your hub URL, sign in, and navigate to <code>/admin/vaults</code>, <code>/admin/users</code>, and <code>/admin/permissions</code>. If those load without 401 or 403, you're set up correctly.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>2. Provision a vault per friend</h2>

        <p>Vaults are private per user. The hub mints per-vault scopes, and the OAuth issuer pins each user's tokens to their assigned vaults. There's no path for one user to read another's vault. Friends can have multiple vaults &mdash; one personal + one family-shared is a common shape &mdash; or just one. Either way, vaults are isolated from each other; only the admin (you) can read all of them.</p>

        <p>Provision via <code>/admin/vaults</code> &rarr; <strong>Add vault</strong>. Give it a name your friend will recognize when they sign in &mdash; their first name or initials work. <code>maya</code>, <code>jp</code>, <code>dad</code>. Lowercase, alphanumeric, dashes and underscores only.</p>

        <p>The vault boots immediately. Check the vault list shows it as <code>running</code> before moving on. (Sending an invite that provisions a <em>new</em> vault? Skip this step &mdash; the vault is created when your friend redeems the link.)</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>3. Send your friend an invite link</h2>

        <p>Go to <code>/admin/users</code> &rarr; <strong>Invite links</strong>. Pick the shape of the invite:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Create a new vault</strong> (the default) &mdash; your friend redeems the link, picks a username and password, and becomes owner of a freshly-provisioned vault. You can pre-name the vault, or let them name it.</li>
            <li><strong>Share an existing vault</strong> &mdash; the link assigns them to one of your hub's vaults at the role you choose: <strong>read-only</strong> or <strong>read &amp; write</strong>. This is the "give Jonathan read-only access to the vault I built" move.</li>
            <li><strong>Pre-name the username</strong> (optional, either shape) &mdash; the redemption form shows it read-only; they only pick a password.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Hit <strong>Create invite</strong>, then <strong>Copy message</strong> &mdash; a ready-to-send note that tells your friend what the link does, with the link included. The link is <strong>one-time and expiring</strong> (7 days by default), and it's shown only once &mdash; the hub stores only a hash, so copy it before you navigate away. An unredeemed invite can be revoked from the same list.</p>

        <p><strong>Send it over a channel you trust.</strong> Signal, an in-person handoff &mdash; not a chat log that lives forever. Anyone holding the link can redeem it (once) before it expires.</p>

        <p><strong>The manual alternative:</strong> <code>/admin/users</code> &rarr; <strong>Create user</strong> still works &mdash; you pick their username, a default password (12+ characters, handed off out-of-band), and their assigned vault(s). They're force-redirected through change-password on first sign-in. Reach for this when you'd rather set everything yourself; the invite link is the smoother default.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>4. What your friend sees</h2>

        <p>They open the invite link, land on a <strong>Claim your invite</strong> page that spells out what they're getting (their username if you pre-named it, the vault and role), pick a password, and are dropped at <code>/account/</code> &mdash; their personal home page. It shows their vault(s) as cards, each with its own <strong>Open Notes</strong> button, and a link to change their password later if they want.</p>

        <p>From then on they sign in at <code>&lt;hub-url&gt;/login</code> with their username and password &mdash; the only things they ever need to remember.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="connect-mcp">
        <h2>5. Connecting a friend's AI client to their vault</h2>

        <p>Notes (the web app) connects with one click via the popover &mdash; nothing to configure. But a friend who wants to drive their vault from <strong>Claude Code, Claude.ai, or any MCP-aware client</strong> connects to a URL, the same as you would. Two facts to hand them:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>The endpoint is <code>&lt;hub-url&gt;/vault/&lt;their-vault&gt;/mcp</code></strong> &mdash; your hub's public URL (the same one they sign in at), their vault name (shown on their <code>/account/</code> page), and the literal <code>/mcp</code> suffix. The suffix matters: the bare <code>/vault/&lt;name&gt;</code> path is a read-only metadata page, not the MCP endpoint, and an MCP client will fail its connection handshake against it. Use the hub's URL, never the vault's internal port.</li>
            <li><strong>Auth is the hub's standard OAuth.</strong> When their client connects, it sends them to your hub to sign in and approve &mdash; their token is pinned to their own vault and can't reach anyone else's. No token-pasting for OAuth-capable clients; for clients that need a bearer header instead, they can mint a hub JWT scoped to their vault from their own access (or you mint and hand one off out-of-band).</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The literal per-client commands &mdash; <code>claude mcp add --transport http &lt;name&gt; &lt;hub-url&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code> for Claude Code, the Settings &rarr; Connectors flow for Claude.ai, and the generic streamable-HTTP shape for everything else &mdash; live in <a href="/install/#connect-mcp-clients">Connecting MCP clients</a> on the install page. Point your friend there; the only substitutions are your hub's URL and their vault name.</p>

        <p><strong>The in-product shortcut:</strong> in your admin SPA, open the Vaults list and hit the <strong>Connect</strong> toggle on the friend's vault row. That expands a <em>Connect an MCP client</em> card with the exact <code>&lt;hub-url&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code> endpoint and a copy-paste <code>claude mcp add</code> command already filled in for that vault &mdash; plus an optional mint-a-token path for headless clients. It's the same surface for every vault, any time; no need to retype the URL by hand.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="onboarding-checklist">
        <h2>Onboarding a team member &mdash; the short version</h2>

        <p>The sections above cover each step in depth. Once you've stood up your hub, this is the whole loop per person:</p>

        <ol>
            <li><strong>Create an invite.</strong> <code>/admin/users</code> &rarr; <strong>Invite links</strong>: new vault (they become owner) or share an existing vault at read-only / read &amp; write; optionally pre-name their username.</li>
            <li><strong>Copy message, send it out-of-band.</strong> The ready-to-send note includes the link and what it grants. Use Signal or an in-person handoff &mdash; the link is one-time and expires in 7 days.</li>
            <li><strong>They redeem it.</strong> They pick a username (unless you pre-named it) and a password, and land at <code>/account/</code>, their home page showing their vault(s).</li>
            <li><strong>They can change their password any time</strong> from <code>/account/</code>. If they forget it, <strong>you reset it</strong> from <code>/admin/users</code> &rarr; <strong>Reset password</strong> (which also revokes their existing tokens).</li>
            <li><strong>To connect their AI client to their vault,</strong> open the Vaults list and use the per-row <strong>Connect</strong> toggle &rarr; <em>Connect an MCP client</em> card, or send them <a href="#connect-mcp">step 5 above</a> / <a href="/install/#connect-mcp-clients">Connecting MCP clients</a>. Notes (the web app) needs none of this &mdash; it connects with one click.</li>
        </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>What your friend can and can't do</h2>

        <p><strong>Can:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Open Notes against any of their assigned vaults &mdash; web app at <a href="https://notes.parachute.computer">notes.parachute.computer</a>, runs on any device. Adds itself as a PWA on iOS and Android. The connect flow lets them switch between vaults they have access to.</li>
            <li>Connect a custom client to their vault &mdash; a custom Surface running on their laptop, an MCP-aware AI client, or any tool that speaks OAuth + MCP. The hub issues per-vault tokens through the standard OAuth flow; their token is pinned to their vault, can't reach anything else. (<a href="#connect-mcp">How to connect an AI client &rarr;</a>)</li>
            <li>Change their own password from <code>/account/change-password</code> any time.</li>
            <li>Sign out from <code>/account/</code>.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Can't:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>See or read any vault outside their assigned list. The OAuth issuer pins their tokens to their assigned vaults; vault servers enforce scope server-side. Friends with two assigned vaults can authorize into either, but never into a third the admin hasn't granted them.</li>
            <li>Reach <code>/admin/</code>. The admin SPA is gated to the first admin (you); anyone else who navigates there gets redirected to <code>/account/</code>.</li>
            <li>Create users, manage vaults, or mint operator-scope tokens. The token-mint endpoints check that the caller is the first admin before issuing.</li>
            <li>Promote themselves or anyone else.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>If a friend wants to build their own Surface</h2>

        <p>This is where multi-user gets interesting. A friend who codes can build their own UI &mdash; a custom Notes-like app, a journaling tool, a research workbench &mdash; that talks directly to their vault on your hub.</p>

        <p>Hand them the <a href="/onboarding/surface-build/">Surface-build starter prompt</a>. It walks an AI client through scaffolding a custom UI on the surface SDK (<code>@openparachute/surface-client</code> + <code>surface-render</code>), wired to the vault's HTTP API via the standard OAuth flow. They'll need:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>Your hub's URL (the same one they use to sign in).</li>
            <li>The vault name(s) shown on their <code>/account/</code> page.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The OAuth flow is standard: their app registers as a client via dynamic client registration (DCR), redirects them to your hub for consent, gets a code, exchanges it for a vault-scoped token. <strong>You don't need to pre-register their client.</strong> External clients hit a consent screen on first authorize, where your friend approves &mdash; once they approve, future authorizes are seamless. If they have multiple assigned vaults, the consent screen shows a picker so they choose which vault to authorize for; the token is pinned to that single choice.</p>

        <p>Tokens are pinned to the vault the friend picked. Their custom Surface can never talk to a vault outside their assigned list, even if they wire it wrong.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>If something goes wrong</h2>

        <p><strong>Friend forgot their password.</strong> Reset it from <code>/admin/users</code>: click <strong>Reset password</strong> on their row, pick a new temp password, hand it to them out-of-band. They'll be force-redirected through change-password on next sign-in, exactly like the original default-password flow. All their existing tokens are revoked automatically as part of the reset.</p>

        <p><strong>Friend's tokens leaked.</strong> Go to <code>/admin/permissions</code> for the per-vault grants view, or <code>/admin/tokens</code> for the per-token registry. Revoke individual tokens or every token for a user. Their OAuth clients will get 401 on next call and re-prompt for consent. The admin password reset above also revokes tokens as a side effect &mdash; useful if you want a clean slate.</p>

        <p><strong>Wrong vault assigned.</strong> Open <code>/admin/users</code>, click <strong>Edit Vaults</strong> on the friend's row, adjust the multi-select, save. Their notes in any removed vault stay put (vaults and assignments are independent), but they lose access via OAuth on the next token mint. Existing access tokens stay valid until they expire (~15 min); refresh tokens stop minting against the removed vault.</p>

        <p><strong>Removing a friend entirely.</strong> Delete the user via <code>/admin/users</code>. Their vaults are <em>not</em> deleted by that action &mdash; vaults and accounts are independent. If you want the vault gone too, delete it via <code>/admin/vaults</code> after.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>What multi-user is not (yet)</h2>

        <p>Honest about the current limits:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>No public signup.</strong> Every account starts with you &mdash; an invite link you issue, or an account you create. There's no "sign up with email," no SSO.</li>
            <li><strong>Roles stop at read-only / read &amp; write per vault.</strong> A shared vault can be granted read-only or read &amp; write (enforced server-side, down to the token scope). There's no finer granularity &mdash; no per-tag or per-note permissions, no delegated admin.</li>
            <li><strong>2FA is opt-in per user.</strong> Anyone can enroll TOTP two-factor from <code>/account/2fa</code> (recommended on a publicly exposed hub), but you can't <em>require</em> it for your users yet.</li>
            <li><strong>No self-service password reset by the user.</strong> Friends can change their password any time at <code>/account/change-password</code>, but if they forget it, you (the admin) have to reset it via <code>/admin/users</code>. Self-service email-based reset comes later.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The original design document is at <a href="/design/2026-05-20-multi-user-phase-1/">/design/2026-05-20-multi-user-phase-1/</a> (invite links and per-vault read-only roles have shipped since it was written). If any of these limits block your use case, open an issue at <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-hub/issues">the hub tracker</a> and we'll prioritize.</p>
    </section>

</main>
